swehouse.c: whole sign and 0 Aries cusps stand still but report a speed

Cusps in the whole sign (W) and 0 Aries (N) systems sit on sign boundaries. They
do not move between ingresses, and then jump 30 degrees. The speed reported for
them was the equal house speed the cusps carried before the boundaries were
imposed on them.

    swetest -b1.1.2000 -ut12:00 -house13.4,52.5,W -speed -sid1

      house  1    0  0  0.0000   600 43 39.5410   ->   0  0  0.0000
      house  2   30  0  0.0000   600 43 39.5410   ->   0  0  0.0000
      house  3   60  0  0.0000   600 43 39.5410   ->   0  0  0.0000

Zero is the honest answer between ingresses. It is not the whole truth, because
the derivative does not exist at an ingress, but 600 degrees per day for a cusp
that has not moved is wrong at every instant rather than at one.

600 is not the worst of it. Over 25,520 dates at random latitudes and longitudes
the largest speed these cusps report is 12,385 degrees per day, and a gate that
judges them on absolute error rather than relative finds the pristine library
returning 12,385.19 for a cusp whose derivative is exactly zero. After this
patch that same gate reads 0.000e+00 for both letters.

Caller risk, which is real here: any code dividing by a cusp speed, or using it
to extrapolate a cusp forward, gets a division by zero where it used to get a
number. That is the argument against this patch, and it is why it is separate
from the rest.

The sidereal path needs its own line and gets one here. It computes 'W' as equal
houses through ihs2 and truncates to the sign boundaries afterwards, so case 'W'
in CalcH() never runs from there and the equal house speeds would otherwise
survive on cusps that have just been pinned.

Positions are untouched.

This is one concern lifted out of "swehouse.c: compute house cusp speeds
analytically" so that it can be taken alone. That patch contains this change and
rewrites the surrounding code as well, so the two cannot both be applied; it is
also available as an eight-step series, of which this is one step. Independent of
every other patch in the set.

---
 swehouse.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+)
diff --git a/swehouse.c b/swehouse.c
index 5172eab..5f750e4 100644
--- a/swehouse.c
+++ b/swehouse.c
@@ -703,6 +703,17 @@ static int sidereal_houses_trad(double tjde,
       cusp[i] = (i - 1) * 30;
     }
   }
+  /* Whole sign and 0 Aries cusps sit on sign boundaries and stand still
+   * between ingresses. Case 'W' in CalcH() zeroes their speeds, but it is
+   * never reached from here: 'W' is computed as equal houses through ihs2
+   * above and the sign boundaries are imposed afterwards, so without this the
+   * equal house speeds would survive on cusps the truncation has just pinned,
+   * which is the defect this patch removes on the tropical path. 'N' is
+   * already zero and is included so the two cannot drift apart. */
+  if ((ihs == 'W' || ihs == 'N') && cusp_speed != NULL) {
+    for (i = 1; i <= ito; i++)
+      cusp_speed[i] = 0;
+  }
   for (i = 0; i < SE_NASCMC; i++) {
     if (i == 2)	/* armc */
       continue;
@@ -1425,6 +1436,10 @@ static int CalcH(
     }
     hsp->do_interpol = hsp->do_hspeed;
     break;
+  /* Whole signs from 0 Aries: the cusps are the twelve sign boundaries and do
+   * not depend on armc at all, so every speed is zero. The code before this
+   * patch left ac_speed on cusp 1 and mc_speed on cusp 10, and the mirroring
+   * carried them to 7 and 4, describing a motion these cusps do not have. */
   case 'N':	/* whole signs, begin at 0° Aries */
     acmc = swe_difdeg2n(hsp->ac, hsp->mc);
     if (acmc < 0) {
@@ -1433,6 +1448,12 @@ static int CalcH(
     }
     for (i = 1; i <= 12; i++) 
       hsp->cusp[i] = (i - 1) * 30.0;
+    /* These cusps are constants, they do not move at all. Without this the
+     * Ascendant and MC speeds set before the switch would be reported for
+     * cusps 1 and 10 (and, through the mirroring below, for 4 and 7). */
+    if (hsp->do_hspeed)
+      for (i = 1; i <= 12; i++)
+	hsp->cusp_speed[i] = 0;
     break;
   case 'O':	/* Porphyry houses */
 porphyry:
@@ -1605,9 +1626,21 @@ porphyry:
       hsp->ac = swe_degnorm(hsp->ac + 180);
       hsp->cusp[1] = hsp->ac;
     }
+    /* Whole sign houses: the cusps sit on sign boundaries and do not move
+     * between ingresses, so every speed is zero. The derivative does not
+     * exist at an ingress and is zero everywhere else; reporting ac_speed
+     * here, as the code did before, described a motion the cusps do not
+     * have. */
     hsp->cusp[1] = hsp->ac - fmod(hsp->ac, 30);
     for (i = 2; i <=12; i++)
       hsp->cusp[i] = swe_degnorm(hsp->cusp[1] + (i-1) * 30);
+    /* The cusps are the start of the Ascendant's sign and fixed multiples of
+     * 30 degrees from it. They stand still while the Ascendant crosses a sign
+     * and jump when it enters the next one, so the derivative is zero
+     * everywhere it exists. */
+    if (hsp->do_hspeed)
+      for (i = 1; i <= 12; i++)
+	hsp->cusp_speed[i] = 0;
     break;
   case 'X': {
     /*
